WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.989 --> 00:00:04.290
WEISMAN: Good morning, ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:13.080
Why don't we begin; we have a full discussion
planned.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:17.760
Welcome to the Council, my name is Steve Weisman.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.900
I'm a former correspondent, among other things,
for The New York Times in South Asia, and

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.861
it's a pleasure to be back at the Council
with so many friends that I can see in the

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:30.000
audience.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.400
And it's a pleasure to preside over this panel,
Integrating the Region and Bridging Differences

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:46.670
in South Asia and China's relationship with
India and Pakistan.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.690
So our-as you can see, I won't make long introductions.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:58.601
Our-we have a great panel: Shahid Javed Burki
is the former finance minister of Pakistan

00:00:58.601 --> 00:01:04.360
and former vice president of the World Bank;
Jayant Prasad, director general, Institute

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:09.490
for Defense Studies and Analyses, and former
Indian ambassador to Afghanistan and Algeria

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:16.830
and Nepal; and Su Xiaohui, who is now associate
research fellow for the Department of International

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:21.579
and Strategic Studies at the China Institute
of International Study.

00:01:21.579 --> 00:01:33.969
So the focus this morning is on the economic
integration and bridging the differences in

00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:35.490
South Asia.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:43.250
And I'm throwing a bit of a curve ball; I'm
going to start with our Chinese participant,

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:55.640
Ms. Su, because I think the economic progress
and integration of the-of the region, everybody

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:06.240
would agree, China is the indispensable focus
right now, certainly from Washington's perspective,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:14.580
with its fascinating ambitions for a major
infrastructure program throughout the region.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:26.670
So let me begin by asking Ms. Su to discuss
your view of China's goals for economic integration

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:35.660
in the region, and to, if you would like,
address whether or not any of the other countries

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:43.400
in the region, and whether the United States,
is reacting constructively to these goals.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:53.630
SU: OK, so first I will begin with China's
initiatives, the so-called Belt and Road initiatives.

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:59.110
We have a lot of names, One Belt, One Road-actually,
there's two parts, and the first part is the

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:03.790
Silk Road Economic Belt, and second part is
the 21st Century Maritime Silk Road.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:07.760
To make it easier, it's called Belt and Road
initiatives in China.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:14.360
Well, it was a big plan initiated by China's
head of the country, President Xi Jinping,

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.800
in the year 2013.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:21.840
And after that, the Chinese people, the economic
people and officials, were asked again and

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.780
again what was China trying to do.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:28.270
And I have to say, that some neighboring countries
got panicked when they heard ideas.

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:34.270
Well, China has some strategic plans in the
region, and there's a lot of consideration

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:35.280
behind ideas.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.190
Well, the first thing I believe China is trying
to do is to boost economic development.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:45.410
Well, in recent years, you may notice that
in President Xi's term, China has been acting

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:48.250
in a different way than in previous years.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.140
It is very obvious.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:57.070
In economic fields, China is trying to make
more (voices ?) over more public goods, and

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.410
in the security fields it is the same.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:04.350
Since the Chinese leadership has said clearly
that we need to address the security issues

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:09.980
in a sustainable way, which means we will
depend on the development of the region.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:17.669
So the economic public goods or the economic
initiatives made by China is a part of China's

00:04:17.669 --> 00:04:19.120
big plans.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:26.479
And the Belt and Road initiatives and the
AIIB, as you may be interested, some interesting

00:04:26.479 --> 00:04:29.590
ideas or big ideas proposed by China.

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:34.999
The first thing I suppose China is trying
to do is to boost original economic development.

00:04:34.999 --> 00:04:41.780
There has been a lot of calculation inside
of China based on the data from the ADB and

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:50.259
other economic sectors, saying that Asia and
a lot of countries may need investments in

00:04:50.259 --> 00:04:52.110
infrastructure construction.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:05:00.330
And it is said from the year 2010 to 2020,
that South Asia may need 2.6 trillion U.S.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:01.569
dollars for the investment.

00:05:01.569 --> 00:05:06.790
And at the same time, China believes that
the investment in infrastructure construction

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:11.509
may have some more effects.

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:17.550
One thing we always talk about is that one
dollar will bring three to four dollars in

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:18.590
other sectors.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:24.439
So this is what they're believing in China,
and this is not a slogan or propaganda.

00:05:24.439 --> 00:05:30.759
You may look at some important documents released
by China's government, like the reports of

00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:36.380
the 18th National Congress of the CPC, China's
Communist Party.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:41.840
It does say clearly that we want to boost
the economic development in the region, and

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:50.120
at the same time, we believe that it is the
way to address the complex problems in the

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.120
neighborhood.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.479
So another thing that I suppose you may be
interested is that China's idea is to boost

00:05:55.479 --> 00:05:58.770
the regional security framework.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:03.610
I don't know whether you follow the news or
not, a couple of weeks ago, the foreign minister

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:09.669
meeting of the CICA, the Conference of Interaction
and Confidence-Building Measures of Asia,

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:10.689
was held in China.

00:06:10.689 --> 00:06:17.069
Well, I have to say that before the year 2014,
when the first leaders meeting was held in

00:06:17.069 --> 00:06:20.930
Shanghai in China, few people know about this
conference.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:25.449
So after that, all of the people were asking
what China is trying to do with this framework.

00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:31.510
It is clear that maybe after two years, China
will host another leaders meeting again.

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:36.820
Well, it is already announced by Chinese government
that we will do it.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:43.030
And so this framework means that China would
like to play a kind of role, these-the design

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:46.180
of the frameworks in the neighborhood.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.939
The first thing is that it is good for all
the countries.

00:06:48.939 --> 00:06:54.569
Well, since the securities have become so
complicated and all the countries need development,

00:06:54.569 --> 00:07:00.849
it is time for China to provide some contribution
to the neighboring countries.

00:07:00.849 --> 00:07:06.930
At the same time, I have to admit that China
believes that the current existing frameworks

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:11.749
may not play a positive role, and may not
play a sufficient role in the neighborhood.

00:07:11.749 --> 00:07:18.360
We all know that for economic development,
there has been efforts of the ABD in Asia,

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:24.020
but it is not enough to support the needs
of all the related countries.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:28.620
And for the security issues, well, it is clear
that China believes that the United States,

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.350
and its allies, does not play a very positive
role in the region.

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:37.270
So China needs to propose some ideas that
differ from the U.S. way and to see whether

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:39.699
the other countries like it or not.

00:07:39.699 --> 00:07:45.330
So it is in the process of trying; it is not
just trying to force the other countries to

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:50.159
accept it, but just provide an option for
the regional countries.

00:07:50.159 --> 00:07:55.550
And another thing is that I don't believe
some traditional security issues will be talked

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:57.259
about in China's framework.

00:07:57.259 --> 00:08:03.020
China still insists that territory disputes
should be talked about, but in the bilateral

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:04.020
ways.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:08.589
So the regional framework, the multilateral
organizations, will focus on something that

00:08:08.589 --> 00:08:14.289
relates to everybody, like the nontraditional
security issues, like terrorism.

00:08:14.289 --> 00:08:18.909
So that is in recent years what China is trying
to do.

00:08:18.909 --> 00:08:27.529
WEISMAN: That was a wonderful presentation
and provides an excellent segue into-for you,

00:08:27.529 --> 00:08:29.770
Ambassador Prasad.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:34.760
Do you see-do you have-is your perspective
identical?

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:40.060
Where do you have concerns, based on what
you've just heard?

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:43.399
PRASAD: Thank you, Steve.

00:08:43.399 --> 00:08:50.000
This was an excellent way to start, beginning
with China, because the transformation economically

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:56.449
that they have brought in a short span of
two generations, lifting 500 (million), 600

00:08:56.449 --> 00:09:02.820
million people out of poverty, is a great
example to other nations in Asia, particularly

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:03.820
India.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:08.270
And our national leadership is often judged
by whether or not we are measuring up to that

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:10.089
standard.

00:09:10.089 --> 00:09:12.480
And Su has well begun.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.420
Not far from this building, at the Bergson
(sp) Institute, we had Arvind Subramanian,

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:19.680
who worked here for several years.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:25.490
He wrote a bestseller which did very well
in the U.S., but did even better in China,

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:29.460
called "Eclipse: Living in the Shadow of China's
Economic Dominance."

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:37.899
Well, he begins the book with a hypothetical
scene of a U.S. president walking into the

00:09:37.899 --> 00:09:45.050
office of the Chinese head of IMF, asking
for a $3 trillion short term bailout for balance

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:46.370
of payments.

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:50.320
So we are starting from that point.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:51.320
(Chuckles.)

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:57.029
We-in China, we have become very friendly
in our conversations.

00:09:57.029 --> 00:10:04.319
And so sometimes, out of-when the guard is
down and India and China are put on the same

00:10:04.319 --> 00:10:12.120
platform, the Chinese-those who are very friendly
to us remind us that China is there and India

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.200
is there.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:15.820
So India has a long way to go.

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:20.709
Looking at the One Belt, One Road initiative,
this is an emblematic Chinese initiative.

00:10:20.709 --> 00:10:27.450
Whether it's driven by internal factors or
not does not matter; the combination of the

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:34.911
two, the SREB Silk Road Economic Belt and
the 21st Century Maritime Silk Road, it's

00:10:34.911 --> 00:10:39.459
cataclysmic in its conception.

00:10:39.459 --> 00:10:46.690
It is Sinocentric because the earlier description
captured the singularity of the proposal by

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:53.550
calling it One Belt, One Road, but it subsumes
within it many other ideas that have been

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:55.589
around for a long time.

00:10:55.589 --> 00:11:02.610
And so it's now rightly called Belt and Road;
we call it in India one belt and many roads,

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:05.480
one belt being the global belt.

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:12.310
You look at India's own connectivity issues:
Because our GDP was low, we didn't invest

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:15.940
in our neighborhood the manner-in the manner
in which we should have.

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:22.910
But we have this Kunming Initiative to connect
Yunnan through Myanmar, through Bangladesh,

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:24.630
to the Port of Calcutta.

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:29.920
We have the Asian Highway, which is being
built, first, the trilateral highway between

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:31.980
Thailand, Myanmar, and India.

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:41.120
We are building roads and railways now in
Bangladesh and in Nepal, five points populated

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:43.509
centers are being connected with India.

00:11:43.509 --> 00:11:47.699
The Chinese are bringing their railway also
into Nepal from Tibet.

00:11:47.699 --> 00:11:55.610
There is the Asian Highway project, which
ADB worked on for a long time, and the Trans-Asian

00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:57.000
Railway project.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:02.880
There was a meeting in September in Delhi
of the St Petersburg group, which is Russia,

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:08.550
Iran and India, who have been joined by nine
other countries for building an international

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:13.880
north-south corridor through Azerbaijan using
Iranian ports.

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:20.009
And next month, we hope to sign a trilateral
agreement between Iran, Afghanistan, and India

00:12:20.009 --> 00:12:25.329
for the development of the Chabahar Port,
which gives Afghanistan a second access to

00:12:25.329 --> 00:12:26.459
the sea.

00:12:26.459 --> 00:12:33.279
So as you see, areas where-in which India
and China can cooperate and collaborate, we

00:12:33.279 --> 00:12:35.399
are not to be found wanting.

00:12:35.399 --> 00:12:38.910
We, with China, set up the New Development
Bank.

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:44.670
It has an Indian CMD; they sit in Shanghai,
and it's supposed to start dispersing funds

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:46.579
in an-in the next month or so.

00:12:46.579 --> 00:12:53.660
We also became a member of the AIIB, and part
of your question was also whether the U.S.

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:56.000
is doing the right thing.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:03.410
Well, the U.S.' singular negative response
to the Chinese initiative was a bit misplaced,

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:09.269
because even with this big economic gap between
India and China-and the contributions to the

00:13:09.269 --> 00:13:20.019
AIIB, for instance, are by a mix of both real-terms
GDP and purchasing power parity GDP-but India

00:13:20.019 --> 00:13:25.959
has ended up being the second largest shareholder,
and with the second largest voting rights.

00:13:25.959 --> 00:13:33.399
And if U.S. had not put pressure on its allies
not to join, so countries like Japan, then

00:13:33.399 --> 00:13:37.139
this would not be described today as a Chinese
bank.

00:13:37.139 --> 00:13:42.330
So this is an example where it shows India
will cooperate with China where we have common

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:43.330
interests.

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:48.779
The problem with the OBOR design from India's
perspective is if you look at all the maps,

00:13:48.779 --> 00:13:54.149
it touches the periphery of India, but does
not cross India.

00:13:54.149 --> 00:14:03.230
And India's great strategic connect will be
to connect inner Asia, Central Asia, Iran,

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:09.399
Afghanistan, to its west, with the GMS region,
which the Chinese have developed so well,

00:14:09.399 --> 00:14:13.879
the Greater Mekong Subregion, Vietnam, Cambodia,
Laos.

00:14:13.879 --> 00:14:19.019
And they have leveraged very cleverly ADB
funding for it: They did the construction

00:14:19.019 --> 00:14:23.970
of the Chinese connectivity projects, and
they got the ADB to build the projects in

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:25.399
these three countries.

00:14:25.399 --> 00:14:28.860
And India is looking for a connect to these
CMLB countries.

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:34.980
We have a special purpose vehicle, financial
vehicle, created to have an industrial connect

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:35.980
there.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:45.900
And finally, I wanted to say that-the OBOR
has an interesting idea that the SREB and

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:51.509
the 21st Century Maritime Silk Road, they
converge in two parts of the globe: They converge

00:14:51.509 --> 00:14:57.500
together the land and the sea belt in Europe
and in Pakistan.

00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:02.100
And so Pakistan's alliance to China changes
fundamentally.

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:08.620
Pakistan's alliance to China so far was to
keep India busy in South Asia.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:14.389
But from now on, it is different, because
Pakistan is going to play a major role in

00:15:14.389 --> 00:15:20.690
Chinese regional and global policies, because
it gives a connectivity from Kashgar to Gwadar;

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:26.629
it opens China greatly into the Arabian Sea
and the Persian Gulf.

00:15:26.629 --> 00:15:34.360
And therefore, there is the good side of it
that if Pakistan makes-has the disposition

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:40.690
to receive this enormous Chinese investment
of 46 (billion dollars), $50 billion in connectivity

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:46.360
and energy projects and transform itself,
that will be good for India, because India

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:50.800
would then be dealing with a stable and self-confident
Pakistan.

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:58.910
But it would not be able to do so unless the-you
know, the inveterate opposition to India is

00:15:58.910 --> 00:15:59.910
taken care of.

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:05.639
And there, I suppose, just as the Chinese
constructively are trying to assist in Afghanistan,

00:16:05.639 --> 00:16:10.130
if they tried also to assist in Pakistan,
that might be a reality.

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:15.779
And the final point, a quick one, is that
India and China are two countries dissimilar

00:16:15.779 --> 00:16:23.350
in comprehensive national power today, but
their self-image of themselves is very similar,

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:29.339
and their attitude to the world, their place
in the world, is very similar.

00:16:29.339 --> 00:16:34.899
And they have been neighbors of long standing,
but with very little physical contact between

00:16:34.899 --> 00:16:35.899
them.

00:16:35.899 --> 00:16:42.209
Itinerant travelers went from India to China;
the sea contact was much greater between Kerala,

00:16:42.209 --> 00:16:47.629
connecting the Arab Countries with China and
Southeast Asia, but in history, there was

00:16:47.629 --> 00:16:52.410
a period when the two countries converged
in Southeast Asia.

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:59.180
And that convergence was not based on conquest;
it was based on culture and commerce.

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:04.569
That is the time of the Sri Vijaya Kingdom
from the eighth to the 12th centuries in the

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:08.260
Malay Peninsula and the Indonesian Archipelago.

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:15.410
There was a Sino-Indian confluence in Southeast
Asia, and for the Chinese project to succeed,

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:22.020
not only what Su referred to, is building
a security architecture in Asia important,

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:25.179
but a Sino-Indian confluence is important.

00:17:25.179 --> 00:17:27.079
WEISMAN: Thank you.

00:17:27.079 --> 00:17:36.960
More -excuse me-more food for thought: You
present a very idealistic view of the prospects

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:45.700
of economic integration in which longtime
rivals in the region will suddenly be holding

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:53.320
hands and building infrastructure to their
mutual benefit.

00:17:53.320 --> 00:18:02.299
Minister Burki or Vice President Burki, is
that really realistic that these projects,

00:18:02.299 --> 00:18:10.779
which, as Ambassador Prasad pointed out, are
concentrated, from India's perspective, on

00:18:10.779 --> 00:18:19.710
its periphery, extending all the way from
East Asia to, you know, ports in Sri Lanka,

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:30.789
and then more infrastructure in southern Pakistan-how
to avoid what Ambassador Prasad just said

00:18:30.789 --> 00:18:43.970
of using-of these-this economic development
aggravating old rivalries in the region, and

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:49.470
ensuring that there's cooperation, and that
these old rivalries can be overcome?

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:55.990
BURKI: Well-(chuckles)-two or three responses
to what Ambassador Prasad said.

00:18:55.990 --> 00:19:02.480
One was that he said India has a long way
to go before it comes anywhere near China

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:09.580
insofar as economic development is concerned,
and I'd like to pick up on that a little bit.

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:17.590
And the second one was it's very interesting
that in response to our Chinese friend's presentation,

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:24.150
he gave us some idea about Indian responses,
this is hard to link Indonesia, Southeast

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:31.610
Asia, through India, Chabahar, Afghanistan,
Iran, and so on, bypassing Pakistan.

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:42.429
Now, my problem is that the BRICS left South
Asia in such a great hurry that they promoted

00:19:42.429 --> 00:19:46.409
nationalism rather than regionalism in South
Asia.

00:19:46.409 --> 00:19:49.900
And I've done some work in this area, and
it's very interesting.

00:19:49.900 --> 00:20:01.010
If you look at South Asia as a complete integrated
entity and you go back to 1947 when the partition

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:08.090
took place, in 1949 when China became what
China is today, you'll find that South Asia

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:17.080
had more advantages than China did: very large
land mass, a tremendously developed agriculture

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:22.610
system spread over the entire length and breadth
of South Asia.

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:26.070
China was very concentrated on the Eastern
seaboard.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:30.929
I worked in China for a very long time, when
as a-when I was at the World Bank, and whenever

00:20:30.929 --> 00:20:35.929
I met with the senior leaders of China, they
would say to me, you've got to remember that

00:20:35.929 --> 00:20:41.490
we have 25 percent of the world's population,
but only 11 percent of the world's cultivable

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:43.200
area.

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:47.080
Indian subcontinent didn't have that kind
of problem.

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:55.240
Now, what happened is that in the Indian subcontinent,
nationalism became the main preoccupation

00:20:55.240 --> 00:21:02.399
of policymakers, and regionalism was totally
discarded as a way of dealing with its own

00:21:02.399 --> 00:21:09.649
economic prospects, and also matching China's
growth.

00:21:09.649 --> 00:21:16.300
If you look at the last 35 years, it's very
interesting that China's GDP has expanded

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:18.429
32 times.

00:21:18.429 --> 00:21:23.990
Chinese economy today is 32 times larger than
what it was in 1980.

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:30.929
The subcontinent's economy, if you combine
the GDPs of mainland South Asia, is only eight

00:21:30.929 --> 00:21:32.020
times.

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:37.630
So that's how far South Asia has been left
behind, and my view is that has happened because

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:43.659
of policies which encourage nationalism rather
than regionalism.

00:21:43.659 --> 00:21:48.860
And if you can somehow overcome that through
political will, then I don't think India has

00:21:48.860 --> 00:21:50.419
a long way to go, Ambassador.

00:21:50.419 --> 00:21:58.940
I think India can go pretty fast if it begins
to act in a regional manner, rather than in

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:00.690
promoting its national interest.

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:07.270
Now, coming to China, it's very interesting
what the Chinese are doing, and I look at

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:15.799
it somewhat differently from what other people
have looked at this One Road, One Belt project.

00:22:15.799 --> 00:22:22.950
My view is that China has decided, given the
fact that their previous development was focused

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:29.049
entirely on the east coast, that they have
to develop their midwest and their western

00:22:29.049 --> 00:22:30.049
part.

00:22:30.049 --> 00:22:35.860
And the only way they can do this is to usher
in a new development paradigm, and in this

00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:43.090
paradigm, land-based commerce is going to
be the most important element.

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:50.370
I was in Astana three or four months ago,
capital of Kazakhstan-and I didn't know this

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:56.720
before I went there-that they have an enormously
large project somewhat similar to what they're

00:22:56.720 --> 00:22:57.909
doing in Pakistan.

00:22:57.909 --> 00:23:05.700
So you have these two parallel roads going
from China to the Arabian Sea through Pakistan

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:12.330
and then several thousand miles north, through
Kazakhstan.

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:19.380
I made the point in Astana that it is very
important to connect these two by north-south

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:25.260
linkages, and the Chinese said-there was a
larger presentation of Chinese at this seminar-and

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:30.330
they came to me and said, you're making a
very interesting point, but Afghanistan has

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:31.970
to settle down.

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:36.919
And this is very much in our plan, and one
of them said to me, you know, the picture

00:23:36.919 --> 00:23:45.720
that we have is kind of a highway system that
the United States has built in its own country,

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:50.740
which connects various parts, and this is
the way we see our role in our western part

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:52.670
in Central Asia.

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:55.380
So my view is that this is a game changer.

00:23:55.380 --> 00:24:01.080
And I think India has to take the leadership
to become a part of this game, rather than,

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:06.660
you know, these things, how do you bypass
Pakistan and so on and so forth, that's a

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:09.789
nationalistic response to what needs to be
done.

00:24:09.789 --> 00:24:17.659
If-and I'm putting onus on India because India
is 80 percent South Asia in terms of its economy

00:24:17.659 --> 00:24:23.090
and population and so on-and we know from
other regional association, unless the anchor

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:32.840
economy shows initiative, shows foresight,
imagination, et cetera, et cetera, no regional

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:33.840
project can take off.

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:36.530
And India, therefore, has to take the lead.

00:24:36.530 --> 00:24:44.190
If it does not, my fear is that this particular
Chinese development paradigm will simply hive

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:47.030
off Pakistan from South Asia.

00:24:47.030 --> 00:24:53.410
And within, my guess is, five to 10 years,
South-Pakistan will become a much more central

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:59.430
player in Central Asia, Western Asia, and
so on, and turn us back towards South Asia.

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:05.840
So that's one take on what I think is happening
at this point.

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:08.160
WEISMAN: Thank you.

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:15.260
Well, you don't have to look at the-further
than the American presidential campaign to

00:25:15.260 --> 00:25:23.050
see that the attitude in Washington is-toward
China's rise is in flux.

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:33.080
And you all referenced the U.S. skepticism
of the Asia infrastructure bank, and perhaps

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:38.800
even of the entire Belt and Road initiative.

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:44.649
I can't be and don't want to be a mouthpiece
for Washington policymakers, but I wonder

00:25:44.649 --> 00:25:52.350
if they were here-and some veterans are in
the audience-if they would wonder if you all

00:25:52.350 --> 00:26:00.750
are being naive about the possibilities of-and
the implications of all of this.

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:07.821
But in particular, I just want to ask you
quickly to assess, in this context, whether

00:26:07.821 --> 00:26:16.460
the U.S. is going about these developments
in the right way, for example, by forming

00:26:16.460 --> 00:26:24.390
its own trading bloc with the Asia Pacific
Region, that includes Japan, but at least

00:26:24.390 --> 00:26:30.870
for now, does not have plans to include China.

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:33.120
Is this a constructive approach?

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:40.440
Why don't you begin, Ms. Su, but let's have,
if you can, some quick answers so we can go

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:41.440
to the floor.

00:26:41.440 --> 00:26:46.000
SU: Well, I have to say that in China, there's
also suspicion about U.S. designs, like TPP.

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:52.510
If you ask the Chinese people, how do you
feel about TPP-well, I suppose if he knows

00:26:52.510 --> 00:26:58.850
what TPP is, he will speak in this way-well,
it is kind of designed by the U.S. to try

00:26:58.850 --> 00:27:06.240
to exclude China from a kind of economic framework,
and so United States is trying to lead the

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:08.580
world over there for economic developments.

00:27:08.580 --> 00:27:14.539
Well, I have to say that there has been suspicion
between China and the United States.

00:27:14.539 --> 00:27:19.490
The problem may be that in recent years, China
has been making a lot of progress, and the

00:27:19.490 --> 00:27:25.080
United States believes that everything China
is trying to do is kind of counterbalanced

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:30.990
against the U.S. approaches, like the AIIB
is something to replace the World Bank and

00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:37.010
the Boao Forum, as you may know, is kind of
an Asia Davos.

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:45.159
And I have to say that the CICA is another
thing like the Shangri-La Dialogue in China,

00:27:45.159 --> 00:27:51.100
and another framework brought up by China
to make China's friends circle.

00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:57.720
So I don't believe it is easy to eliminate
all the suspicion or concerns from both sides,

00:27:57.720 --> 00:28:03.020
but what China is trying to do is to try to
reach out to the other parts of the world,

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:09.049
including the United States, to make the other
countries believe that China is not trying

00:28:09.049 --> 00:28:12.149
to-driving out the United States out of Asia.

00:28:12.149 --> 00:28:15.799
The first thing is the cooperation between
the AIIB and the World Bank.

00:28:15.799 --> 00:28:23.580
I heard the president of AIIB talking about
the cooperation months ago, so it is something

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:31.190
that when the AIIB was in the process of establishment,
the president was thinking about to try to

00:28:31.190 --> 00:28:36.840
cooperate with other institutions, like World
Bank, in the world.

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:43.190
And now, we have seen that they are moving
together and we will witness the process possibly

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:48.230
in May this year-in May or in June this year.

00:28:48.230 --> 00:28:49.940
Another thing is about the Silk Road.

00:28:49.940 --> 00:28:55.500
I know that the United States also has a Silk
Road; it's called New Silk Road project.

00:28:55.500 --> 00:29:00.799
I remember years ago, when it was not called
Silk Road, it was called Great Central Asia,

00:29:00.799 --> 00:29:02.120
or something like that.

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:07.200
In China, there has been a lot of discussion
on this project, saying that the United States

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:13.909
was trying to do something really big in Central
Asia, in South Asia, and trying to make it

00:29:13.909 --> 00:29:15.500
a strategic plan.

00:29:15.500 --> 00:29:21.649
But after calculations, both in the academic
field and in the official departments, what

00:29:21.649 --> 00:29:26.059
China believes that it might not be totally
bad news for China.

00:29:26.059 --> 00:29:31.169
It is-development, it is something good for
every party, every related party, and China

00:29:31.169 --> 00:29:35.809
would like to play some role in the-in the
New Silk Road.

00:29:35.809 --> 00:29:38.990
And it is the same, like the Belt and Road
initiative.

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:44.741
It is a big plan, and China believes that
if the United States would like to be part

00:29:44.741 --> 00:29:46.279
of it, it is good news.

00:29:46.279 --> 00:29:52.010
I remember last year, a delegation from Department
of State visited my institute, talking about

00:29:52.010 --> 00:29:53.169
the Belt and Road initiative.

00:29:53.169 --> 00:29:57.130
They asked a lot of questions about what we
are trying to do.

00:29:57.130 --> 00:30:03.049
And the good thing is that we heard people
from the delegation speaking clearly that

00:30:03.049 --> 00:30:09.370
the United States would think about how to
link the two things together and to make cooperation

00:30:09.370 --> 00:30:12.919
or coordination between two big plans.

00:30:12.919 --> 00:30:17.460
So this is a starting point, even though a
lot of my colleagues said that it would be

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:23.549
something really difficult to achieve, but
to have a starting point and to make people

00:30:23.549 --> 00:30:29.070
think about it is something really good, not
only for China, but also good for the United

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:30.159
States.

00:30:30.159 --> 00:30:35.330
So what China is trying to do recently is
that China is trying to explain to the outside

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:41.029
world that the development of China is good
news, not bad news, to the other countries.

00:30:41.029 --> 00:30:45.919
And the belt road initiative, I have to say,
that is not totally new ideas.

00:30:45.919 --> 00:30:49.270
We talk about China-Pakistan economic corridor.

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:55.280
It is something that happened-already happened
previously to the Belt and Road initiative.

00:30:55.280 --> 00:31:00.580
So the Belt and Road initiative is not the
presidency's idea that China would like to

00:31:00.580 --> 00:31:08.049
do some big plan and to change the world totally;
it is based on something that already existed,

00:31:08.049 --> 00:31:09.860
already happening.

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:18.649
And China is trying to coordinate every part
of what we're doing into a big plan that will

00:31:18.649 --> 00:31:20.980
try to benefit as much countries as possible.

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:22.149
WEISMAN: Thanks.

00:31:22.149 --> 00:31:30.212
Ambassador Prasad, is the-let's see if we
can do this quickly-is the U.S. approaching

00:31:30.212 --> 00:31:31.490
this the right way?

00:31:31.490 --> 00:31:36.460
PRASAD: Well, it's an unfair question to ask
a non-American-(scattered laughter)-because-

00:31:36.460 --> 00:31:43.259
WEISMAN: I know, Indian diplomats are very
reluctant to criticize U.S. policy.

00:31:43.259 --> 00:31:44.429
(Scattered laughter.)

00:31:44.429 --> 00:31:50.799
PRASAD: No, but before I come there and answer
your question in a more direct way on TPP

00:31:50.799 --> 00:31:55.200
versus RCEP, I want to comment-to have a comment
on that.

00:31:55.200 --> 00:31:59.279
I wanted to respond to Minister-Mr. Burki.

00:31:59.279 --> 00:32:05.610
He very rightly says the onus is on India,
and the Indians now, I think, beyond-this

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:12.039
is-there is a consensus in India between the
present government and the former one that

00:32:12.039 --> 00:32:16.880
India has to lift its game, not just in South
Asia, but Asia as a whole.

00:32:16.880 --> 00:32:23.440
The problem is that the Indian plans right
now are the second best plans because of Pakistan's

00:32:23.440 --> 00:32:28.269
obduracy on not allowing overland access through
Pakistan.

00:32:28.269 --> 00:32:33.759
And the Chinese project-no Chinese leader
or Pakistani leader has told us that aside

00:32:33.759 --> 00:32:42.450
from the north-south axis of Kashgar to Gwadar,
they are even conceding of, in theory, a possible

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:47.950
east-west axis, which connects India to CAREC,
the Central Asia Regional Economic Cooperation

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:52.880
area, which is connected with China and with
Pakistan, of course.

00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:58.929
So our first preference, very clearly, would
be to connect to Pakistan, with Pakistan,

00:32:58.929 --> 00:33:04.480
and inside, we don't just have non-tariff
measures, but Pakistan has this non-tariff

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:09.929
area which is country-specific, which doesn't
allow India MFN treatment.

00:33:09.929 --> 00:33:16.821
But on RCEP and TPP, I have this to say, that
the preferred regional framework for USA is

00:33:16.821 --> 00:33:24.630
very clearly U.S. dominated and in the present
context, seems to be geared against China.

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:30.409
And RCEP is a response, but not just Chinese
response, because it brings together the five

00:33:30.409 --> 00:33:37.690
biggest economies of Asia: China, India, Japan,
South Korea and Australia.

00:33:37.690 --> 00:33:40.990
They account together for a third of global
GDP.

00:33:40.990 --> 00:33:47.590
They account for 28 percent of merchandise
trade and 23 percent of disembodied trading

00:33:47.590 --> 00:33:48.620
services.

00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:51.909
So it's going to be a significant development.

00:33:51.909 --> 00:34:01.529
And India is dramatically altering its foreign
trade policies, from a mercantilist way of

00:34:01.529 --> 00:34:03.659
looking reducing tariffs.

00:34:03.659 --> 00:34:09.010
For example, in India, the average tariffs
are 13 percent, which is in very few countries

00:34:09.010 --> 00:34:10.470
of the world.

00:34:10.470 --> 00:34:17.080
And there, India has accepted, in principal,
two fundamental new principles, which are

00:34:17.080 --> 00:34:18.720
being proposed in RCEP.

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:23.369
One is called ratcheting, and the other is
called MFN-forward.

00:34:23.369 --> 00:34:29.450
Ratcheting means that if there are any domestic
policy reforms, they would automatically,

00:34:29.450 --> 00:34:34.389
in terms of national treatment, apply for
RCEP member states.

00:34:34.389 --> 00:34:39.520
And MFN-forward means that if India were to
enter into, let's say, a free-trade agreement

00:34:39.520 --> 00:34:45.770
with the United States, then whatever concessions
India offers to the United States would automatically

00:34:45.770 --> 00:34:49.100
also apply to RCEP member countries.

00:34:49.100 --> 00:34:51.300
So there is great promise in this.

00:34:51.300 --> 00:34:57.180
We have run into a very serious negotiating
problem: the Chinese are, of course, backing

00:34:57.180 --> 00:35:03.360
our position, and the Australians on-but some
others are not-of allowing Mode 3 and Mode

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:05.410
4 access.

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:10.950
The ASEAN has a problem, because the ASEAN
10 are part of negotiations, they don't have,

00:35:10.950 --> 00:35:14.640
in the new economic union that they have created,
free trading services.

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:21.869
India is asking that in return for reducing
its very high tariffs, they must have access

00:35:21.869 --> 00:35:27.160
in the services area, both for Indian companies
to establish themselves overseas, but also

00:35:27.160 --> 00:35:31.300
for professional and worker exports.

00:35:31.300 --> 00:35:32.580
And so that's a stumbling block.

00:35:32.580 --> 00:35:38.830
And the other slight stumbling block, which
can be overcome, is on the IPRs issue, where

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:41.180
India and China are on the same page.

00:35:41.180 --> 00:35:43.170
So this is interesting.

00:35:43.170 --> 00:35:47.150
WEISMAN: Sir, Mr. Burki-
BURKI: Yes.

00:35:47.150 --> 00:35:51.490
WEISMAN: What-just comment, but briefly, so
we can go to the floor.

00:35:51.490 --> 00:35:52.490
BURKI: Right.

00:35:52.490 --> 00:35:58.900
I think United States has made two very serious
mistakes in the last few years.

00:35:58.900 --> 00:36:06.750
U.S., after all, was the founder of the Bretton
Woods system, and-a system that it began to

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:11.619
dominate after the collapse of Soviet Union
in 1991.

00:36:11.619 --> 00:36:17.089
It should not have backtracked; it should
have persevered, and that would have meant

00:36:17.089 --> 00:36:23.670
giving a greater share to China, India, and
other large economies that were kept out of

00:36:23.670 --> 00:36:28.940
the Bretton Woods system in 1944-45, because
they were very small in those days, and U.S.

00:36:28.940 --> 00:36:31.230
did not do this.

00:36:31.230 --> 00:36:41.020
And when I was at the Bank-and you will remember
this also-capital shares were being constantly-share

00:36:41.020 --> 00:36:49.170
of capital of various countries was constant-subject
of constant debate between the Bank management

00:36:49.170 --> 00:36:53.910
and the large U.S.- and U.S. authorities.

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:55.810
And U.S. was not prepared to give much.

00:36:55.810 --> 00:36:58.530
It gave a little bit, but not much.

00:36:58.530 --> 00:37:05.440
Second big mistake that U.S. has made, I think,
is to let WTO go.

00:37:05.440 --> 00:37:10.800
There was a meeting in Nairobi, I think, in
November, where they decided to more or less

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:12.010
kill WTO.

00:37:12.010 --> 00:37:20.780
Now, trade is a very difficult subject: you-all
you have to do is to listen to Mr. Donald

00:37:20.780 --> 00:37:26.130
Trump to realize what kind of emotional reactions
that trade can begin.

00:37:26.130 --> 00:37:29.390
Bretton Woods' two institutions were created
immediately after the conference.

00:37:29.390 --> 00:37:36.359
The third leg took 50 years to do-to create:
WTO.

00:37:36.359 --> 00:37:42.589
But then WTO was abandoned, and in its place,
U.S. has taken the leadership to split the

00:37:42.589 --> 00:37:49.240
world into two very large trading blocs, and
that is an effort to keep China out of both.

00:37:49.240 --> 00:37:53.910
And I think that's a very big mistake, and
what we are now seeing is government officials

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:59.750
within the local-within the global system
and U.S.-the point I was making about India

00:37:59.750 --> 00:38:03.980
regionally, I would make about the United
States globally.

00:38:03.980 --> 00:38:12.030
U.S. is not declining; U.S. will remain the
most important economic power in the world

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:13.829
for a long time to come.

00:38:13.829 --> 00:38:19.030
But it has to behave as one, and that means
providing leadership and not taking this easy

00:38:19.030 --> 00:38:25.060
way out, finish off WTO and we'll do TPP and
transatlantic pact, and so on and so forth.

00:38:25.060 --> 00:38:30.119
So I think mistakes have been made, and U.S.
has to think long, rather than short.

00:38:30.119 --> 00:38:36.380
Just one point about thinking long: She said
that CPEC is something that developed quickly

00:38:36.380 --> 00:38:39.359
over the last couple of years; that's not
the case.

00:38:39.359 --> 00:38:46.300
I had a conversation with the Chinese prime
minister in 1994, and he took me to his office,

00:38:46.300 --> 00:38:48.400
and he showed me the map of China.

00:38:48.400 --> 00:38:51.940
And he said, do you find anything strange
about this map?

00:38:51.940 --> 00:38:55.540
And I had no idea what he was talking about,
so I said, you tell me.

00:38:55.540 --> 00:39:01.099
And he said, we are the only large country
that is landlocked on three sides.

00:39:01.099 --> 00:39:03.640
We are open only on one side.

00:39:03.640 --> 00:39:08.900
And we want to open to the West, and we want
to go through Pakistan, we want to build a

00:39:08.900 --> 00:39:11.720
corridor, but Pakistan is taking no interest.

00:39:11.720 --> 00:39:16.230
If you know somebody senior in Pakistan, can
you communicate this message to them?

00:39:16.230 --> 00:39:19.130
So they-China thinks long.

00:39:19.130 --> 00:39:27.069
Unfortunately, sitting in the U.S. capital,
I'm prepared to say the U.S. thinks short.

00:39:27.069 --> 00:39:31.160
WEISMAN: OK, let's go to questions.

00:39:31.160 --> 00:39:32.599
Sir.

00:39:32.599 --> 00:39:37.440
And please introduce yourself-
Q: I'm Tim-

00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:42.090
WEISMAN: -and if I may, ask that you ask a
question.

00:39:42.090 --> 00:39:44.260
Q: Tim Pietrai (sp).

00:39:44.260 --> 00:39:49.829
Looking at intervening the region from the
bottom up instead of from the top down, Dr.

00:39:49.829 --> 00:39:55.081
Fred Starr at SAIS, who studied the region,
did a survey of truck drivers, the tremendous

00:39:55.081 --> 00:39:57.180
traffic already in the region.

00:39:57.180 --> 00:40:03.740
And their largest problem wasn't terrorism
or anything else, or bad roads, it was corruption,

00:40:03.740 --> 00:40:06.640
from governments largely, crossing borders.

00:40:06.640 --> 00:40:13.810
Is there anything that is being done on a
regional basis to not just lower tariffs,

00:40:13.810 --> 00:40:20.700
but to lower non-legal barriers to trade?

00:40:20.700 --> 00:40:23.300
WEISMAN: Which of you would like to pick up
on that?

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:26.910
BURKI: I can-I can start-
WEISMAN: None of the countries here are involved

00:40:26.910 --> 00:40:27.910
in corruption.

00:40:27.910 --> 00:40:34.670
BURKI: I'd like to respond to Ambassador Prasad's
comment that-I know India's been seeking access

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:39.390
through Pakistan, Afghanistan, and so on,
and Pakistan is not prepared to do that-my

00:40:39.390 --> 00:40:42.890
view is, never take a Pakistani no as the
final answer.

00:40:42.890 --> 00:40:44.210
(Scattered laughter.)

00:40:44.210 --> 00:40:45.829
You have to press Pakistan.

00:40:45.829 --> 00:40:52.160
And if you do analysis-something I have done-Pakistan
benefits much more from giving transit rights

00:40:52.160 --> 00:40:55.000
to India to Afghanistan than India does.

00:40:55.000 --> 00:41:00.960
And that message has to be given to Islamabad
and Pakistan relentlessly.

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:10.010
I was troubled when I saw the agenda of the
last two meetings between the senior leaders.

00:41:10.010 --> 00:41:12.710
Trade connectivity was not mentioned at all.

00:41:12.710 --> 00:41:17.750
It was all same thing, Kashmir-(inaudible)-and
so on and so forth, and terrorism.

00:41:17.750 --> 00:41:28.609
On terrorism, there is an exaggerated view
of what's going on in the United States in

00:41:28.609 --> 00:41:29.609
Pakistan.

00:41:29.609 --> 00:41:35.710
Whenever I go to Pakistan, my American friends
tell me, please, look after yourself.

00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:41.559
And I say to them that the probability of
my being killed in a terrorist attack in Pakistan

00:41:41.559 --> 00:41:47.450
is insignificant compared to the probability
of my dying on New Jersey Turnpike of a road

00:41:47.450 --> 00:41:48.450
accident.

00:41:48.450 --> 00:41:49.450
(Chuckles.)

00:41:49.450 --> 00:41:54.220
And so why there is so much play?

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:55.570
And his answer was very interesting.

00:41:55.570 --> 00:42:00.680
He said, if you get killed in a terrorist
attack, you will make it to the front page

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:03.420
of New York Times with your picture.

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:11.940
If it is death in a New Jersey Turnpike accident,
your wife will have to pay to buy that space.

00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:17.730
Because of connection between Islam and terrorism,
there is a lot of focus on what's happening

00:42:17.730 --> 00:42:19.069
in Pakistan.

00:42:19.069 --> 00:42:25.280
And the terrorists, small group of people,
marginal to the society, know this.

00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:33.079
It's very interesting that when there was
this attack on a public park in Lahore, the

00:42:33.079 --> 00:42:37.790
terrorist who took responsibility said, we
attacked Christians.

00:42:37.790 --> 00:42:38.880
Why did they say that?

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:43.720
Because they knew if they say this, they would
be on the front page of every newspaper.

00:42:43.720 --> 00:42:49.180
That day, I was traveling from Pakistan to
the United States, and I stopped in Dubai

00:42:49.180 --> 00:42:54.520
and picked up a foreign newspaper: Every newspaper
from Australia to the United States had that

00:42:54.520 --> 00:42:55.550
on the thing.

00:42:55.550 --> 00:43:00.480
So let's not get overwhelmed by this problem
posed by terrorism.

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:01.770
It is horrible.

00:43:01.770 --> 00:43:02.770
It will be solved.

00:43:02.770 --> 00:43:08.589
The Chinese have accepted the Pakistani offer
to create a special unit of the Pakistan Army

00:43:08.589 --> 00:43:13.400
to provide them with a sense of protection,
and they are doing this.

00:43:13.400 --> 00:43:18.950
And when I was in Pakistan last time, I talked
to some of the folks who were doing this.

00:43:18.950 --> 00:43:24.760
WEISMAN: I'm going to take the prerogative
of going to the next question.

00:43:24.760 --> 00:43:26.280
Yes.

00:43:26.280 --> 00:43:30.750
Q: Islam Siddiqui, SAIS senior adviser.

00:43:30.750 --> 00:43:31.762
A comment and a question.

00:43:31.762 --> 00:43:34.770
I think-comment about the WTO.

00:43:34.770 --> 00:43:41.390
WTO, I must say, that is doing well; it's
the Doha, which was killed essentially in

00:43:41.390 --> 00:43:42.550
Nairobi and not the WTO.

00:43:42.550 --> 00:43:48.109
If you talk to Director General Azev�do,
he will say all of the other functions of

00:43:48.109 --> 00:43:49.109
WTO are going fine.

00:43:49.109 --> 00:43:50.109
WEISMAN: I think you make a good point.

00:43:50.109 --> 00:43:51.359
Would you like to ask a question, though?

00:43:51.359 --> 00:43:52.359
Q: Yes.

00:43:52.359 --> 00:43:53.359
I will.

00:43:53.359 --> 00:43:56.210
That's my-question to Ambassador Prasad.

00:43:56.210 --> 00:44:02.500
The tariffs in your region, not only in India,
but in that general-is still, you can call,

00:44:02.500 --> 00:44:04.089
13 percent average.

00:44:04.089 --> 00:44:09.550
But the-if you look at the (water ?) between
the-(inaudible)-and applied trade, what's

00:44:09.550 --> 00:44:13.690
being done to reduce the tariffs so that they
come closer to applied?

00:44:13.690 --> 00:44:16.559
PRASAD: Sorry, I didn't get the question properly.

00:44:16.559 --> 00:44:18.520
Q: So, tariffs, I think you said, are about
13 percent.

00:44:18.520 --> 00:44:19.520
PRASAD: Oh, that part, OK.

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:25.050
Q: But do you have a-boundaries are still-that's
the-that's the impasse in WTO negotiations.

00:44:25.050 --> 00:44:30.050
PRASAD: Right, well, I'll take advantage of
your question to also answer the preceding

00:44:30.050 --> 00:44:31.150
one.

00:44:31.150 --> 00:44:38.369
Actually, India is engaged in a massive project
in South Asia of building regional public

00:44:38.369 --> 00:44:45.780
goods, beginning with initiatives on trade,
transportation, information, and communication

00:44:45.780 --> 00:44:46.780
technologies.

00:44:46.780 --> 00:44:52.650
Most important is energy, and the World Bank
has carried out a study that 90 percent of

00:44:52.650 --> 00:45:01.440
the cooperative potential on energy lies with
four northeastern states of Bhutan, Nepal,

00:45:01.440 --> 00:45:08.190
India, and Bangladesh, because India and Bangladesh
are power hungry; there is a huge hydro potential

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:11.560
in both Bhutan and Nepal.

00:45:11.560 --> 00:45:14.660
And the last connectivity bit is people to
people.

00:45:14.660 --> 00:45:18.740
So you can build infrastructure, but if you
don't have the superstructure, if you don't

00:45:18.740 --> 00:45:24.859
have facilitation, if you have difficult borders,
if borders are barriers, not bridges, then

00:45:24.859 --> 00:45:26.090
all of this is waste.

00:45:26.090 --> 00:45:28.329
So your question is a very valid one.

00:45:28.329 --> 00:45:30.550
We have to address that issue.

00:45:30.550 --> 00:45:36.550
We have done-we have looked at an ADB Institute
study that just came out two, three months

00:45:36.550 --> 00:45:42.119
ago, which says that India-ASEAN trade has
galloped faster than India's trade with other

00:45:42.119 --> 00:45:50.180
parts of the world, in the same way that five,
10 years ago, the India-China trade galloped

00:45:50.180 --> 00:45:58.680
and multiplied 30 times in 10 years from 2
billion (dollars) in 2000 to 62 billion (dollars)

00:45:58.680 --> 00:46:00.079
in 2010.

00:46:00.079 --> 00:46:05.640
Now, the India-ASEAN trade and economic exchanges
are valued at around $100 billion annually.

00:46:05.640 --> 00:46:13.560
The study says that if the tariffs are going
to be down as a result of RCEP, we also have

00:46:13.560 --> 00:46:20.599
FTAs with a number of ASEAN countries now,
but if there is a 50 percent reduction in

00:46:20.599 --> 00:46:29.070
non-tariff measures and 15 percent improvement
in reducing logistics cost, then the India-ASEAN

00:46:29.070 --> 00:46:35.250
exchanges can go up by a factor of five, which
means that in terms of trade and investments,

00:46:35.250 --> 00:46:39.680
the gains would be five (hundred billion dollars)
to $600 billion annually.

00:46:39.680 --> 00:46:47.079
So I think the tariff issue will be sorted
out if the negotiations in-under RCEP are

00:46:47.079 --> 00:46:51.920
concluded, let's say, by the end of this year
or the middle of next year.

00:46:51.920 --> 00:46:54.290
WEISMAN: We'll go here.

00:46:54.290 --> 00:46:56.070
Q: Thank you.

00:46:56.070 --> 00:46:58.440
Hani Findakly, Clinton Group.

00:46:58.440 --> 00:47:03.760
I just want to follow up on Shahid Burki's
last comment about the U.S. moving away from

00:47:03.760 --> 00:47:04.760
multilateralism.

00:47:04.760 --> 00:47:12.600
The fact is that the U.S. was the biggest
beneficiary of multilateralism-Marshall Plan,

00:47:12.600 --> 00:47:18.690
Bretton Woods system and WTO-and yet in the
last 20, 25 years, the U.S. has moved into

00:47:18.690 --> 00:47:21.570
more regionalism and more bilateral agreements.

00:47:21.570 --> 00:47:27.260
And despite the fact that, neither politically
nor economically, have these agreements have

00:47:27.260 --> 00:47:32.000
shown any evidence of moving the needles and
toes of the participants' economic growth

00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:35.620
and in terms of the-in terms of the trajectory
growth.

00:47:35.620 --> 00:47:41.040
So the question I have is, given the fact
that there is little political value for these

00:47:41.040 --> 00:47:46.430
agreements, and little-and little economic
value for participants, why do you think the

00:47:46.430 --> 00:47:47.799
U.S. has moved away from that?

00:47:47.799 --> 00:47:56.500
And what should be done to move that dialogue
back into multilateralism?

00:47:56.500 --> 00:48:05.221
WEISMAN: Well-(chuckles)-I don't know that
we want to get into a long discussion of why

00:48:05.221 --> 00:48:11.690
the WTO and the Doha Round failed.

00:48:11.690 --> 00:48:21.700
Would anyone like to voice a view about whether
the U.S. was to blame for killing off the

00:48:21.700 --> 00:48:27.119
multilateralism, or should we-would you like
to address it quickly?

00:48:27.119 --> 00:48:30.089
BURKI: Well, very quickly.

00:48:30.089 --> 00:48:36.720
I wasn't saying that the U.S. is solely responsible
for killing multilateralism, but U.S. has

00:48:36.720 --> 00:48:38.299
been shortsighted.

00:48:38.299 --> 00:48:45.710
I think it was a mistake for U.S. not to give
some serious consideration to the concerns

00:48:45.710 --> 00:48:53.290
of countries, like India, that were legitimately
saying that all the previous rounds were focused

00:48:53.290 --> 00:48:59.340
on lowering tariffs between developed and
developing countries and among developed countries.

00:48:59.340 --> 00:49:05.589
And-but certain sensitive sectors in the United
States and Japan and Europe were protected

00:49:05.589 --> 00:49:09.830
through all kinds of means, subsidies and
so on-agriculture is a case in point.

00:49:09.830 --> 00:49:15.589
India was pressing very hard for that, and
nobody in the United States was prepared to

00:49:15.589 --> 00:49:16.869
give into that.

00:49:16.869 --> 00:49:21.569
And that, I think, ultimately, the Indians
and I guess other people walked out.

00:49:21.569 --> 00:49:27.030
Your point that a lot of things are still
happening in WTO, that's absolutely right.

00:49:27.030 --> 00:49:33.609
I wasn't saying that WTO, as an organization,
is dead; I'm saying that WTO, in order to

00:49:33.609 --> 00:49:39.200
keep moving, had to have success in Doha Round,
and that has been killed.

00:49:39.200 --> 00:49:42.110
WEISMAN: OK, we'll go-
SU: May I-may I?

00:49:42.110 --> 00:49:43.200
WEISMAN: Yes, please.

00:49:43.200 --> 00:49:48.390
SU: From China's perspective, that there is
some driving force behind the changes of the

00:49:48.390 --> 00:49:54.869
U.S. behavior, one thing we believe that is
the United States is trying to concentrate

00:49:54.869 --> 00:49:56.000
on important things.

00:49:56.000 --> 00:50:00.880
Well, in recent years in China, we still believed
that the United States is the only superpower

00:50:00.880 --> 00:50:05.930
in the world, the most powerful country in
the world, and China cannot compete against

00:50:05.930 --> 00:50:11.089
the United States in many fields, like economic,
military, political strengths, everything.

00:50:11.089 --> 00:50:17.720
But at the same time, we noticed that there
has been some adjustment of the U.S. behavior.

00:50:17.720 --> 00:50:24.030
One thing is that United States cannot spread
so much as it did in previous years, like

00:50:24.030 --> 00:50:25.840
in Asia-
WEISMAN: What?

00:50:25.840 --> 00:50:27.210
SU: Spread its strengths.

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:28.250
WEISMAN: Oh.

00:50:28.250 --> 00:50:30.480
SU: It's not that powerful.

00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:31.480
WEISMAN: Right.

00:50:31.480 --> 00:50:36.599
SU: Like in Asia Pacific, the United States
rely on its allies to burden the responsibility.

00:50:36.599 --> 00:50:42.809
Well, it is a sign that the United States
is focusing on some important things and like

00:50:42.809 --> 00:50:46.760
its friends or partners to help on the other
things.

00:50:46.760 --> 00:50:52.349
For the economic framework, I believe that
the political will is really important.

00:50:52.349 --> 00:50:58.480
Look at TPP: What I believe is that behind
the TPP, there is very strong political will

00:50:58.480 --> 00:51:02.010
from the United States and other partners.

00:51:02.010 --> 00:51:08.780
If behind the WTO, there is such strong political
will, I don't believe it will be that.

00:51:08.780 --> 00:51:15.970
WEISMAN: Do you think that when the United
States says, oh, the TPP is setting a framework

00:51:15.970 --> 00:51:23.421
that China eventually-would welcome China's
participation in the TPP to expand it, to

00:51:23.421 --> 00:51:26.380
include China, do you take at face value?

00:51:26.380 --> 00:51:32.510
SU: Well, I have to say that it is positive
news for China, because at the very beginning,

00:51:32.510 --> 00:51:37.089
my understanding is that the United (States)
obviously excluded China out of this framework.

00:51:37.089 --> 00:51:40.270
Now, the United States is talking in a more
polite way.

00:51:40.270 --> 00:51:41.270
WEISMAN: (Laughs.)

00:51:41.270 --> 00:51:46.900
SU: It's that-not shut the door, but you need
to meet the standards to enter into this new

00:51:46.900 --> 00:51:47.900
club.

00:51:47.900 --> 00:51:52.630
So what China's trying to do is to try to
study what the United States is trying to

00:51:52.630 --> 00:52:01.599
do in this circle, and how you perform the
regulations and the all kinds of standards

00:52:01.599 --> 00:52:06.130
like the other countries, and then China will
decide whether to go into it or not.

00:52:06.130 --> 00:52:11.299
And I have to say that in recent years, there
has been discussion in China, widely divided,

00:52:11.299 --> 00:52:16.650
one party saying that, well, it is not a good
club to join in, so let's just make something

00:52:16.650 --> 00:52:17.650
up ourselves.

00:52:17.650 --> 00:52:23.390
And the other party is that, we need to prepare
for it; it is the trend of the economic development;

00:52:23.390 --> 00:52:27.190
one day, China needs to meet this kind of
standards.

00:52:27.190 --> 00:52:32.710
But up to now, I don't see any clear decision
from the upper level on this point.

00:52:32.710 --> 00:52:33.810
WEISMAN: Right.

00:52:33.810 --> 00:52:36.130
There are two questions in the back.

00:52:36.130 --> 00:52:40.380
Why don't you ask both of them-we'll take
both of them and then wind up.

00:52:40.380 --> 00:52:41.380
MS.

00:52:41.380 --> 00:52:43.319
: Great, we can start here.

00:52:43.319 --> 00:52:47.200
Q: I'm Marshall Bouton, Asia Society Policy
Institute.

00:52:47.200 --> 00:52:54.260
I guess I would like to address the question
particularly to Mr. Burki and Ms. Su.

00:52:54.260 --> 00:53:02.859
And you both spoke about, respectively, the
onus being on India and South Asia to improve

00:53:02.859 --> 00:53:04.800
the environment for regional cooperation.

00:53:04.800 --> 00:53:10.020
Ms. Su spoke that-basically said that China's
objective is fundamentally the same as that.

00:53:10.020 --> 00:53:13.710
I would like to understand more about Pakistan's
point of view.

00:53:13.710 --> 00:53:21.940
It seemed to me you dismissed any responsibility
on Pakistan's part a shade very, very rapidly,

00:53:21.940 --> 00:53:27.609
and yet, Pakistan has really been the reluctant
partner in the Indian treaties to open the

00:53:27.609 --> 00:53:29.760
east-west corridor.

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:33.920
And Ms. Su, I would particularly be interested
in why China would invest such a very large

00:53:33.920 --> 00:53:40.130
sum in a north-south corridor without indicating
its own preferences for a greater integration

00:53:40.130 --> 00:53:43.830
of South Asia, if that is, in fact, China's
objective?

00:53:43.830 --> 00:53:51.130
WEISMAN: And let's go to the gentleman there,
and if you could ask a succinct question.

00:53:51.130 --> 00:53:52.130
Q: Sure.

00:53:52.130 --> 00:53:54.859
My name is Alexander Slater, and I work at
the World Bank Group.

00:53:54.859 --> 00:53:59.460
The panel is called Integrating the Region
and Bridging Differences, but I didn't hear

00:53:59.460 --> 00:54:04.680
anything from the panelists on how to bridge
differences that are non-economic, especially

00:54:04.680 --> 00:54:11.290
social differences, issues of inclusion, of-based
on social groups, both within countries and

00:54:11.290 --> 00:54:13.880
sort of social groups that sit across borders.

00:54:13.880 --> 00:54:19.089
I'd love to hear more about ideas that you
have for bridging those differences in order

00:54:19.089 --> 00:54:20.930
to integrate the region.

00:54:20.930 --> 00:54:24.560
WEISMAN: OK, well, we can take 30 seconds
for that, I'm sure.

00:54:24.560 --> 00:54:25.560
(Laughter.)

00:54:25.560 --> 00:54:32.690
Go ahead, let's go down in this direction,
and then we'll-and these will be the concluding

00:54:32.690 --> 00:54:33.690
remarks.

00:54:33.690 --> 00:54:41.240
BURKI: OK, I'm here to talk-ask any person
about where Pakistan stands on a lot of issues.

00:54:41.240 --> 00:54:47.200
It's not a fair question in the sense that
Pakistan is very confused at this point, where

00:54:47.200 --> 00:54:50.570
it wants to go, where it is, and where does
it want to end up.

00:54:50.570 --> 00:54:57.119
And the reason for this is that the country
is developing critically-and thank God, it

00:54:57.119 --> 00:54:58.450
is developing critically.

00:54:58.450 --> 00:55:06.270
It is introducing a system of democracy which-at
least to my way of reading-is unique with

00:55:06.270 --> 00:55:08.240
the western part of the Muslim world.

00:55:08.240 --> 00:55:12.970
There is no other country which is making
the kind of critical progress that Pakistan

00:55:12.970 --> 00:55:13.970
is making.

00:55:13.970 --> 00:55:19.049
I used to say Turkey is another one, but Turkey
is slipping back, whereas Pakistan is going

00:55:19.049 --> 00:55:20.049
forward.

00:55:20.049 --> 00:55:25.280
And in that kind of situation, the Pakistanis
really haven't had the time to think about

00:55:25.280 --> 00:55:27.600
the kind of issues that I'm talking about.

00:55:27.600 --> 00:55:36.180
That's why my view is that the countries where
there is capacity to do thinking on a continuous

00:55:36.180 --> 00:55:42.390
basis-New Delhi is a case in point-should
press Pakistan, should take the initiative,

00:55:42.390 --> 00:55:49.640
and be more forgiving and be more aggressive
in a-in a different sense of the word to get

00:55:49.640 --> 00:55:53.900
Pakistan on board on some of the things that
we are talking about.

00:55:53.900 --> 00:56:03.960
WEISMAN: Ms. Su, and of course, the question
about the north-south corridor-I mean, the

00:56:03.960 --> 00:56:11.619
common interests of China and Pakistan have
really existed throughout-starting after World

00:56:11.619 --> 00:56:13.750
War II and beginning of the Cold War.

00:56:13.750 --> 00:56:24.119
So you can see that there's still this concern
that it's a north-south integration, and not

00:56:24.119 --> 00:56:25.119
east-west.

00:56:25.119 --> 00:56:26.240
It's an interesting question.

00:56:26.240 --> 00:56:33.150
SU: Well, have you ever read the roadmap released
by the Chinese government last year in March,

00:56:33.150 --> 00:56:35.020
talking about Belt and Road initiative?

00:56:35.020 --> 00:56:41.420
Well, it was a very long one, but answering
a lot of questions about Belt and Road initiative.

00:56:41.420 --> 00:56:47.920
The roadmap, it is a document, it is based
upon the research and preparation of a lot

00:56:47.920 --> 00:56:53.390
of departments inside China, including the
MFA, the foreign ministry.

00:56:53.390 --> 00:57:01.369
And it is said that the Belt and Road initiative
is partly for China's external cooperation,

00:57:01.369 --> 00:57:05.550
but for a large part, it is for domestic development.

00:57:05.550 --> 00:57:09.980
If you have been to China, you will know that
the eastern part and the western part are

00:57:09.980 --> 00:57:12.180
quite different from each other.

00:57:12.180 --> 00:57:17.690
For the western part, the Chinese government
has been investing many energies for years

00:57:17.690 --> 00:57:24.420
to try to improve people's living conditions
to boost economic development, but I suppose

00:57:24.420 --> 00:57:29.760
that we haven't achieved what we had planned.

00:57:29.760 --> 00:57:36.559
So the Belt and Road initiative is a comprehensive
design for every part of China to try to play

00:57:36.559 --> 00:57:40.940
its role and to reach its achievement in the
future.

00:57:40.940 --> 00:57:45.750
And for the western parts, I believe that
the cooperation with the other countries like

00:57:45.750 --> 00:57:53.340
Pakistan is a way to boost economic cooperation
and to boost the domestic development of the

00:57:53.340 --> 00:57:54.410
western part.

00:57:54.410 --> 00:57:58.240
So it is a larger part of the consideration
from the Chinese government.

00:57:58.240 --> 00:58:05.549
It is not totally for strategic interests
or just for friendship with our old friends.

00:58:05.549 --> 00:58:07.250
So this is my understanding.

00:58:07.250 --> 00:58:15.760
And for the Belt and Road initiative-it is
a multilateral design; it is not only build

00:58:15.760 --> 00:58:23.809
one belt and many roads, and it is something
that's beyond the infrastructure construction,

00:58:23.809 --> 00:58:30.840
like China would like to reduce the barrier
of trade, like to boost people-to-people exchange.

00:58:30.840 --> 00:58:35.609
That is the thing that-try to narrow the gap.

00:58:35.609 --> 00:58:40.500
WEISMAN: Ambassador Prasad, make some final
comments.

00:58:40.500 --> 00:58:46.930
And I'm afraid the last question was so interesting
and open-ended, we'll not be able to do it

00:58:46.930 --> 00:58:50.990
justice, but why don't you wind things up?

00:58:50.990 --> 00:58:57.230
PRASAD: Well, I'll address the completely
last question on inclusive growth.

00:58:57.230 --> 00:59:04.190
You see, India's pursuit of the inclusive
aspect of growth brings that into a kind of

00:59:04.190 --> 00:59:10.160
confrontation with the rest of the global
community, because China has brought about

00:59:10.160 --> 00:59:16.210
inclusiveness through a different method-which
was partly forced, which we can't do; we have

00:59:16.210 --> 00:59:19.109
to work through democratic processes.

00:59:19.109 --> 00:59:24.030
And this is the main stumbling block, which
the United States did not understand when

00:59:24.030 --> 00:59:29.160
we took a line on the livelihood of the issue
related to agriculture.

00:59:29.160 --> 00:59:35.119
Once U.S. negotiators understood this, there
was a tradeoff immediately, and after quiet

00:59:35.119 --> 00:59:40.730
negotiations between what we were seeking
and trade facilitation.

00:59:40.730 --> 00:59:44.640
But the larger question that you raise is
a very relevant one.

00:59:44.640 --> 00:59:49.630
Of course, the Asians accuse the World Bank
of turning away from infrastructure, which

00:59:49.630 --> 00:59:56.119
is why ADB and AIIB have a certain traction
in the imagination of Asians, because World

00:59:56.119 --> 00:59:59.349
Bank now does mostly social stuff.

00:59:59.349 --> 01:00:01.660
But that social aspect is important.

01:00:01.660 --> 01:00:07.829
Climate change, for example, affects lives,
and it affects three major things: it affects

01:00:07.829 --> 01:00:11.950
water, it affects energy, and it affects food.

01:00:11.950 --> 01:00:17.569
And in South Asia, for example, including
China-because three of the most massive rivers

01:00:17.569 --> 01:00:24.440
of South Asia originate in China: the Indus,
the Sutlej that flow westward into Pakistan

01:00:24.440 --> 01:00:31.220
through India, and the Brahmaputra, which
takes a curve around the Arakans and flows

01:00:31.220 --> 01:00:38.650
into Bangladesh -we haven't even talked-started
talking about management of the Himalayan

01:00:38.650 --> 01:00:39.650
ecosystem.

01:00:39.650 --> 01:00:45.319
There is a big slow shift, an unmistakable
shift in the monsoons; the glaciers are melting.

01:00:45.319 --> 01:00:51.000
It's going to affect not just South Asian
economies, but potentially the global economy.

01:00:51.000 --> 01:00:56.520
We are not talking of joint river managements;
we don't share river data around the-between

01:00:56.520 --> 01:00:58.920
upper riparians and lower riparians.

01:00:58.920 --> 01:01:05.359
And so I'm ending on a sober note that we
have-there is this great global shift from

01:01:05.359 --> 01:01:13.930
the north and the west to the south and the
east, which explains, a little bit, U.S. diffidence

01:01:13.930 --> 01:01:18.980
and its turning away from its own genius in
building international institutions, including

01:01:18.980 --> 01:01:20.520
the World Bank.

01:01:20.520 --> 01:01:29.319
But I think we all have to work cooperatively
in these very difficult areas, some of which

01:01:29.319 --> 01:01:31.940
I'm afraid we haven't addressed.

01:01:31.940 --> 01:01:33.980
WEISMAN: That's a beautiful summary.

01:01:33.980 --> 01:01:35.119
Thank you very much.

01:01:35.119 --> 01:01:40.250
I think we can all agree that this was a great
panel.

01:01:40.250 --> 01:01:49.440
Asia is in desperate need of infrastructure;
how we get there is one of the great challenges

01:01:49.440 --> 01:01:51.970
that we all face.

01:01:51.970 --> 01:01:56.309
And if I may so-say so, we can probably all
agree that the U.S. might be doing a better

01:01:56.309 --> 01:01:59.750
job in building international institutions.

01:01:59.750 --> 01:02:02.779
Maybe some people in the audience will disagree.

01:02:02.779 --> 01:02:07.890
But a panel like this really contributes to
everybody's understanding toward those goals.

01:02:07.890 --> 01:02:13.779
And I want to thank you very much, and thank
the Council for inviting me to moderate today.

01:02:13.779 --> 01:02:15.410
And meeting adjourned.

01:02:15.410 --> 01:02:16.410
PRASAD: Thank you.

01:02:16.410 --> 01:02:17.410
(Applause.)

01:02:17.410 --> 01:02:17.411
(END)

